# Self-Service Printing Platform
## React Native + FastAPI + MySQL + Linux File Storage + Flask Print Agent + CUPS

---

## 1. Project Overview

This project is a self-service printing platform where users upload documents from a React Native mobile application, select their printing options, make payment through Razorpay, enter an OTP at the printing station, and receive their printed documents.

The complete system is hosted and operated using a single Ubuntu/Linux machine.

### Main technologies

```text
Mobile App       → React Native
Backend          → FastAPI
Database         → MySQL
File Storage     → Linux PC HDD / filesystem
Payment          → Razorpay
Print Agent      → Flask
Printer Control  → CUPS
Reverse Proxy    → Nginx
```

### Services NOT used

```text
Firebase                  ❌
Firestore                 ❌
Firebase Authentication   ❌
Cloudinary                ❌
Amazon S3                 ❌
Cloud Storage             ❌
React Web Hosting         ❌
```

The React Native application runs on the user's phone. It does not need to be hosted as a website.

---

# 2. Main User Workflow

The most important workflow of this project is:

```text
APP OPEN
   ↓
UPLOAD / SELECT FILE
   ↓
FILE UPLOADED TO FASTAPI
   ↓
FILE STORED ON LINUX HDD
   ↓
PRINT OPTIONS PAGE
   ↓
USER SELECTS PRINT OPTIONS
   ↓
BACKEND VALIDATES THE JOB
   ↓
JOB ACCEPTED?
   │
   ├── NO
   │    ↓
   │  Show reason
   │
   └── YES
        ↓
     CALCULATE PRICE
        ↓
     PAYMENT PAGE
        ↓
     RAZORPAY PAYMENT
        ↓
     PAYMENT VERIFIED BY BACKEND
        ↓
     PRINT JOB QUEUED
        ↓
     OTP PAGE
        ↓
     USER ENTERS OTP AT PRINT STATION
        ↓
     BACKEND VERIFIES OTP
        ↓
     CORRECT USER + CORRECT FILE + CORRECT JOB
        ↓
     PRINT JOB RELEASED
        ↓
     FLASK PRINT AGENT
        ↓
     CUPS
        ↓
     PHYSICAL PRINTER
        ↓
     PAPER PRINTED
        ↓
     JOB COMPLETED
```

---

# 3. Important Design Principle

The **backend is the source of truth**.

The React Native app only requests operations.

It must NOT decide:

```text
Whether a job is accepted
Whether payment is successful
Whether OTP is correct
Whether a print job is allowed
Whether a print job is completed
Whether a refund is required
```

Those decisions are made by FastAPI.

---

# 4. Complete System Architecture

```text
                         USER
                          │
                          ▼
                ┌──────────────────┐
                │  React Native    │
                │      App         │
                └────────┬─────────┘
                         │
                         │ HTTPS
                         ▼
              ┌──────────────────────┐
              │       Nginx          │
              │   Reverse Proxy      │
              └──────────┬───────────┘
                         │
                         ▼
              ┌──────────────────────┐
              │       FastAPI        │
              │    Main Backend      │
              └───────┬──────┬───────┘
                      │      │
             ┌────────┘      └───────────────┐
             ▼                               ▼
      ┌─────────────┐               ┌─────────────────┐
      │    MySQL    │               │  Linux HDD      │
      │             │               │                 │
      │ Users       │               │ Original files  │
      │ Orders      │               │ PDF             │
      │ Payments    │               │ JPG             │
      │ Jobs        │               │ PNG             │
      │ OTP         │               └─────────────────┘
      └─────────────┘
                      │
                      ▼
              ┌──────────────────┐
              │   Flask Agent    │
              │ Print Controller │
              └────────┬─────────┘
                       │
                       ▼
                    ┌──────┐
                    │ CUPS │
                    └──┬───┘
                       │
                       ▼
                    PRINTER
```

---

# 5. Application Opening Workflow

When the React Native application opens:

```text
React Native App
      ↓
Check authentication
      ↓
Load initial application state
      ↓
Show Upload File screen
```

The first major user action is:

```text
Upload / Select File
```

Example:

```text
┌──────────────────────────────┐
│      SELF PRINTING           │
│                              │
│      Select your file        │
│                              │
│     [ Upload File ]          │
│                              │
│     PDF / JPG / PNG          │
└──────────────────────────────┘
```

---

# 6. File Upload Workflow

When the user selects a file:

```text
React Native
      │
      │ multipart/form-data
      ▼
POST /api/documents/upload
      │
      ▼
FastAPI
```

FastAPI performs:

```text
1. Authenticate user
2. Validate file
3. Validate file size
4. Validate file type
5. Validate actual file content
6. Generate document ID
7. Generate secure stored filename
8. Save file to Linux HDD
9. Calculate SHA-256 checksum
10. Determine page count
11. Store document metadata in MySQL
12. Return document information
```

---

# 7. Physical File Storage

The original uploaded file is stored on the Linux machine.

Recommended:

```text
/var/lib/printplatform/storage/
```

Example:

```text
/var/lib/printplatform/storage/
└── documents/
    └── user_42/
        └── 7c91d2e8.pdf
```

MySQL stores only the metadata and storage key.

Example:

```text
storage_key:
documents/user_42/7c91d2e8.pdf
```

The actual file remains on the Linux HDD.

---

# 8. Why MySQL Does Not Store the File

The system separates:

```text
Structured Data
        ↓
      MySQL
```

from:

```text
Original File
        ↓
Linux HDD
```

MySQL stores:

```text
Document ID
User ID
Original filename
Stored filename
Storage key
MIME type
File size
SHA-256
Page count
Status
Created time
```

The actual PDF/JPG/PNG remains on the Linux filesystem.

---

# 9. After Upload: Print Options Page

After successful upload:

```text
Upload successful
       ↓
Backend returns document ID
       ↓
React Native opens Print Options screen
```

Example options:

```text
Document:
project.pdf

Pages:
1-10

Copies:
2

Colour:
B/W
Colour

Sides:
Single-sided
Double-sided

Paper:
A4

Orientation:
Portrait
Landscape
```

The exact options should be determined by the printer capabilities configured in the backend.

---

# 10. Print Options Request

The app sends:

```http
POST /api/orders/validate
```

Example:

```json
{
  "documentId": "doc_001",
  "printServerId": "PRINT-SERVER-001",
  "pageRange": "1-10",
  "copies": 2,
  "colour": false,
  "sides": "two-sided-long-edge",
  "paperSize": "A4",
  "orientation": "portrait"
}
```

---

# 11. Backend Job Validation

This is a critical step.

The backend must decide whether the requested print job is acceptable.

The backend checks:

```text
1. Is the user authenticated?
2. Does the document exist?
3. Does the document belong to the user?
4. Is the document active?
5. Does the physical file exist?
6. Is the file valid?
7. Is the requested page range valid?
8. Is the number of copies valid?
9. Is the requested paper size supported?
10. Is colour printing supported?
11. Is duplex printing supported?
12. Is the printer online?
13. Is the print server online?
14. Is the requested job within configured limits?
15. Is the document still within its retention period?
16. Is the requested print configuration valid?
```

Only after all required checks pass:

```text
JOB ACCEPTED
```

---

# 12. Job Validation Result

### Accepted

```json
{
  "accepted": true,
  "documentId": "doc_001",
  "pageCount": 10,
  "printablePages": 10,
  "estimatedPrice": 26.00,
  "currency": "INR"
}
```

The mobile application then moves to:

```text
Payment Page
```

### Rejected

```json
{
  "accepted": false,
  "error": "PRINTER_UNAVAILABLE",
  "message": "The selected printer is currently unavailable."
}
```

The mobile application stays on the print-options page and displays the reason.

---

# 13. Important Rule

Do not allow:

```text
Frontend says:
"Job accepted"
```

The backend must explicitly return:

```text
accepted = true
```

The frontend only displays the backend decision.

---

# 14. Price Calculation

Once the job is accepted, FastAPI calculates the final price.

Example:

```text
Pages = 5
Copies = 2
Rate = ₹2/page

5 × 2 × ₹2
= ₹20
```

If additional charges exist:

```text
Printing cost
+ service fee
+ other configured fees
= final amount
```

The price is calculated by the backend.

The frontend must not be trusted to provide the final amount.

---

# 15. Payment Page

Only after the backend accepts the print job:

```text
Job accepted
      ↓
Price calculated
      ↓
Create payment order
      ↓
Razorpay
      ↓
React Native Payment Page
```

Endpoint:

```http
POST /api/payments/create
```

The backend creates the Razorpay payment order.

The mobile app then opens Razorpay checkout.

---

# 16. Payment Verification

The important rule is:

```text
Frontend payment callback
        ≠
Final payment confirmation
```

The backend must verify the Razorpay webhook.

Workflow:

```text
User pays
   ↓
Razorpay
   ↓
Razorpay Webhook
   ↓
FastAPI
   ↓
Verify signature
   ↓
Verify amount
   ↓
Verify order
   ↓
Mark payment successful
```

Only then should the print job move forward.

---

# 17. Payment Failure

If payment fails:

```text
PAYMENT_FAILED
```

The job must NOT be released for printing.

The user can return to the appropriate payment/error page.

The backend must not generate a printable job simply because the mobile app says that payment succeeded.

---

# 18. After Successful Payment

Once the backend receives and verifies successful payment:

```text
Payment successful
       ↓
Create print job
       ↓
Generate OTP
       ↓
Job = WAITING_FOR_OTP
```

The mobile app now moves to:

```text
OTP Page
```

---

# 19. OTP Page

Example:

```text
┌──────────────────────────────┐
│       PRINT JOB READY        │
│                              │
│ Enter the OTP at the         │
│ printing station             │
│                              │
│ OTP: 483921                  │
│                              │
│ Status: Waiting for OTP      │
└──────────────────────────────┘
```

The OTP identifies the user's paid print job.

The OTP should be unique enough within the active OTP set and must be securely generated.

---

# 20. Multiple Users and FCFS

This is an important part of the architecture.

Suppose three users submit jobs:

```text
User A → File A → OTP 123456
User B → File B → OTP 789012
User C → File C → OTP 456789
```

The backend creates a queue:

```text
QUEUE
│
├── Job A
├── Job B
└── Job C
```

The default queue order is:

```text
First accepted/paid job
        ↓
Second accepted/paid job
        ↓
Third accepted/paid job
```

This is **FCFS — First Come, First Served**.

However, the OTP does NOT mean:

```text
"Print any file matching this OTP."
```

The OTP is linked to exactly one print job.

---

# 21. OTP-to-Job Mapping

Example database relationship:

```text
User A
   │
   └── Order A
          │
          └── Print Job A
                 │
                 └── OTP 123456
```

User B:

```text
User B
   │
   └── Order B
          │
          └── Print Job B
                 │
                 └── OTP 789012
```

Therefore:

```text
OTP 123456 → ONLY Job A
OTP 789012 → ONLY Job B
```

The agent must never select a file based only on queue position.

It must use the backend-authorized job identity.

---

# 22. FCFS Queue + OTP Relationship

The recommended behavior is:

```text
                         PRINT QUEUE
                              │
                ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
                ▼             ▼             ▼
             Job A         Job B         Job C
             OTP A         OTP B         OTP C
                │             │             │
                ▼             ▼             ▼
            User A         User B         User C
```

Jobs are ordered FCFS.

But the OTP is used to authorize the correct job.

### Example

```text
Queue:

1. Job A
2. Job B
3. Job C
```

User B enters OTP B.

The system identifies:

```text
OTP B → Job B
```

The backend must not print Job A merely because Job A is first in the queue.

Instead, the station can only release a job when the OTP matches that job.

---

# 23. Important Queue Decision

There are two different concepts:

### Queue order

Determines which jobs are normally processed first:

```text
A → B → C
```

### OTP authorization

Determines which specific paid job the person is authorized to release:

```text
OTP A → A
OTP B → B
OTP C → C
```

These should not be mixed together.

---

# 24. Recommended Station Behavior

When a user reaches the print station:

```text
User enters OTP
       ↓
Flask Agent
       ↓
FastAPI
       ↓
Find job by OTP
       ↓
Validate OTP
       ↓
Validate job
       ↓
Check station/print-server ownership
       ↓
Check job state
       ↓
Check whether job is allowed to print
```

If valid:

```text
JOB → RELEASED
```

If invalid:

```text
REJECT
```

---

# 25. FCFS Handling at the Printer

For physical printer safety, the backend should maintain a queue.

Recommended logic:

```text
PAID jobs are placed in FCFS order.

Only jobs that are:
    - paid
    - valid
    - assigned to this printer
    - OTP-authorized
    - not already printing
can be released.

The printer processes one physical job at a time unless the printer
configuration explicitly supports safe concurrent queue submission.
```

This prevents two users from physically interfering with the same printer.

---

# 26. Example: Multiple Users

Suppose:

```text
10:00:01 → User A uploads File A
10:00:05 → User B uploads File B
10:00:08 → User C uploads File C
```

After option validation and payment:

```text
10:01 → Job A PAID
10:02 → Job B PAID
10:03 → Job C PAID
```

Queue:

```text
1. Job A
2. Job B
3. Job C
```

OTP mapping:

```text
OTP-A → Job A
OTP-B → Job B
OTP-C → Job C
```

If User B enters OTP-B:

```text
OTP-B
   ↓
Job B
   ↓
Verify
   ↓
Release Job B
```

The system must not accidentally print:

```text
Job A
```

just because Job A was first in the queue.

---

# 27. Print Release Workflow

The actual release flow is:

```text
User enters OTP
       ↓
Flask Agent
       ↓
POST /api/agent/release
       ↓
FastAPI
       ↓
Find OTP
       ↓
Find associated order
       ↓
Find associated print job
       ↓
Validate user/job/station relationship
       ↓
Validate OTP
       ↓
Validate job state
       ↓
Mark OTP consumed
       ↓
Mark Job RELEASED
       ↓
Return job authorization
       ↓
Flask Agent
       ↓
Read local file
       ↓
Send to CUPS
```

---

# 28. OTP Must Be Consumed Atomically

Two requests could arrive at almost the same time.

Example:

```text
Request 1 → OTP 123456
Request 2 → OTP 123456
```

Only one request must succeed.

Use a MySQL transaction and row locking.

Conceptually:

```text
BEGIN TRANSACTION

SELECT OTP
FOR UPDATE

Check:
    active
    not expired
    not used
    attempts valid

Mark:
    used_at = NOW()
    active = false

Mark:
    job = RELEASED

COMMIT
```

The second request then sees:

```text
OTP already used
```

and is rejected.

---

# 29. Print Agent Workflow

The Flask agent does not independently decide what to print.

It follows FastAPI's authorization.

```text
Flask Agent
     ↓
Poll FastAPI
     ↓
Get authorized job
     ↓
Resolve storage key
     ↓
Read local file
     ↓
Apply validated print settings
     ↓
Submit to CUPS
     ↓
Monitor CUPS
     ↓
Report status to FastAPI
```

---

# 30. Local File Access

The agent receives:

```text
documents/user_42/7c91d2e8.pdf
```

It resolves:

```text
/var/lib/printplatform/storage/documents/user_42/7c91d2e8.pdf
```

The agent must ensure the resolved path remains inside:

```text
/var/lib/printplatform/storage/
```

This prevents path traversal attacks.

---

# 31. Printing

After successful OTP authorization:

```text
FastAPI
   ↓
Job RELEASED
   ↓
Flask Agent
   ↓
Read local document
   ↓
CUPS
   ↓
Printer
```

The agent applies only the print options already validated by FastAPI.

Example:

```text
Pages
Copies
Colour/B&W
Duplex
Paper size
Orientation
```

---

# 32. Print Job State Machine

Recommended:

```text
CREATED
   ↓
PAID
   ↓
QUEUED
   ↓
WAITING_FOR_OTP
   ↓
RELEASED
   ↓
PRINTING
   ↓
COMPLETED
```

Failure paths:

```text
QUEUED
   ↓
FAILED

WAITING_FOR_OTP
   ↓
EXPIRED

PRINTING
   ↓
FAILED
```

A failed paid job can then enter the refund workflow.

---

# 33. Order State Machine

```text
CREATED
   │
   ▼
VALIDATED
   │
   ▼
PAYMENT_PENDING
   │
   ├──────────────► PAYMENT_FAILED
   │
   ▼
PAID
   │
   ▼
WAITING_FOR_OTP
   │
   ├──────────────► EXPIRED
   │
   ▼
RELEASED
   │
   ▼
PRINTING
   │
   ├──────────────► COMPLETED
   │
   └──────────────► FAILED
                         │
                         ▼
                      REFUND
```

---

# 34. Queue Database Design

A print job should contain fields such as:

```text
print_jobs
---------------------------------------
id
order_id
document_id
print_server_id
queue_position
status
priority
created_at
paid_at
released_at
started_at
completed_at
failed_at
cups_job_id
retry_count
last_error
```

For FCFS, the backend can use:

```text
paid_at
```

or a dedicated sequence number.

A dedicated queue sequence is often safer for deterministic ordering.

Example:

```text
queue_sequence = 1001
queue_sequence = 1002
queue_sequence = 1003
```

---

# 35. FCFS Rule

The default queue ordering should be:

```text
Earlier successfully accepted/paid job
            ↓
processed before
Later successfully accepted/paid job
```

Do not use:

```text
File upload time alone
```

as the final queue order if payment has not yet succeeded.

A user who uploads a file but never pays should not block paid print jobs.

Therefore the recommended FCFS point is:

```text
Successful payment / job queue creation
```

---

# 36. Payment and Queue Ordering

Example:

```text
User A uploads at 10:00
User B uploads at 10:01

User B pays at 10:02
User A pays at 10:05
```

The paid queue becomes:

```text
10:02 → User B
10:05 → User A
```

Therefore:

```text
Job B → first
Job A → second
```

This prevents unpaid uploads from occupying the printing queue.

---

# 37. What Happens If User Uploads But Does Not Pay?

The file can remain temporarily stored.

The order remains:

```text
PAYMENT_PENDING
```

After a configured expiration time:

```text
PAYMENT_PENDING
      ↓
EXPIRED
      ↓
Document cleanup
```

The file is not placed into the physical print queue until payment is successfully verified.

---

# 38. What Happens If Payment Succeeds But User Never Enters OTP?

The job remains:

```text
WAITING_FOR_OTP
```

until:

```text
OTP expires
```

Then:

```text
WAITING_FOR_OTP
       ↓
EXPIRED
       ↓
Refund workflow
       ↓
File cleanup
```

The exact OTP expiry and refund policy should be configurable.

---

# 39. What Happens If Printer Is Offline?

If the printer is offline:

```text
Job validation
      ↓
Printer unavailable
      ↓
Job rejected
```

If the printer becomes unavailable after payment:

```text
PAID
 ↓
WAITING_FOR_OTP
 ↓
Printer unavailable
```

The backend must prevent release if the printer cannot safely accept the job.

If a failure happens after release/printing has started:

```text
PRINTING
   ↓
FAILED
   ↓
Failure handling
   ↓
Refund if appropriate
```

---

# 40. File Cleanup

A file must not be deleted while it is required by a print job.

Recommended:

```text
Upload
  ↓
ACTIVE
  ↓
PAID
  ↓
PRINTING
  ↓
COMPLETED
  ↓
CLEANUP_PENDING
  ↓
DELETE FILE
```

For failed/expired jobs:

```text
FAILED / EXPIRED
       ↓
CLEANUP_PENDING
       ↓
DELETE FILE
```

Before deleting:

```text
Check no active job references the document.
```

---

